# SRG-APP-000148-AU-002270  -  CAT I
# The Central Log Server must be configured to uniquely identify and
# authenticate organizational users (or processes acting on behalf of
# organizational users).
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000148-AU-002270
group:           V-206460
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000764
control_rev5:    IA-2

contribution:    Splunk's authentication.conf selects which authentication system the
                 application uses and names the strategy stanza that carries it, so
                 interactive logons resolve through a directory service or SAML identity
                 provider that issues one identifier per person rather than through a
                 shared native account.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] authType in LDAP|SAML
check:           authentication.conf [authentication] authSettings non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] authSettings https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] authType https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The LDAP directory service or SAML identity provider issues the unique
                 user identifiers and enforces uniqueness in its own account store
external:        Absence of shared or group logon accounts, and retention of a single
                 emergency account of last resort, is evidenced in the Splunk user store
                 ($SPLUNK_HOME/etc/system user database) and in the identity provider, not
                 in a configuration file
external:        The ldap.conf TLS settings named in SPLK-CL-000320 belong to the OpenLDAP
                 client configuration shipped with the operating system layer, outside
                 Splunk's own conf layer
external:        Operating system accounts used for shell or CLI access to the host

citation:        SPLK-CL-000320 check and fix text; authentication.conf.spec:26-55
                 (authType vocabulary and default Splunk, authSettings as the strategy
                 key), authentication.conf.spec:351-360 (role map per strategy);
                 authorize.conf.spec:67-78 (roles are named per role stanza)
finding:         Splunk authentication.conf does not select an external authentication
                 system with a named strategy ([authentication] authType is not LDAP or
                 SAML, or authSettings is empty), so the application does not bind logons
                 to uniquely identified organizational users to the extent the product
                 allows.
